.TH SMVIEW 1
.SH NAME
smview \- project a part into the lines of a drawing view
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smview
[
.B \-h
]
.I view part.fold
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Smview
writes one view of a part, in mm, x to the right and y up,
one record per line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
view VIEW PART
thick T
box X0 Y0 X1 Y1
fill, then m X Y, l X Y, a CX CY R A0 A1, z
line KIND VIS X0 Y0 X1 Y1
arc KIND VIS CX CY R A0 A1
hole X Y D [csink DC ANGLE near|far]
radius R
bend X0 Y0 X1 Y1 ANGLE
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I View
is
.BR front ,
.BR back ,
.BR left ,
.BR right ,
.BR top ,
.B bottom
or
.BR flat .
The front view is seen along +y with z up, the top view along \-z with
y up, and the others in third-angle projection.
The flat view is the top view of the unfolded blank, with a centre line
and a
.B bend
record for each bend (the angle positive toward the eye).
.PP
A fill begins a region of material, filled by the even-odd rule.
Arcs are counterclockwise, angles in degrees.
.I Kind
is
.B e
(sharp edge),
.B i
(edge of a cut-out),
.B o
(rim of a hole, or arc partly hidden),
.B b
(edge or outline of a bend),
.B s
(seam where a bend leaves a flat face) or
.B c
(centre line);
.I vis
is
.B v
or
.BR h .
There is a hole record for each hole seen along its axis and not
covered.
.B Radius
is the inside radius of the first bend.
.TP
.B \-h
Include hidden lines.
.SH SEE ALSO
smdim(1), smdraw(1), fold(5)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A circle seen at an angle (an ellipse) cannot be written; the input is
refused, with the view and the place of the circle.
